## Environment

- Use what the machine already has. Do not add runtimes, package managers,
  compilers, toolchains or services. The platform and the host application
  versions the project supports are listed in its README.
- Add a dependency only when the task cannot reasonably be done with what is
  already declared, record it in the project's declarations, and say why in the
  change. Prefer a few lines of plain code over a new package.
- When the existing environment cannot support the work, or has a clear
  ceiling, stop and put it to the user with the options. Do not install your
  way around it.
- Build only what the current task needs. No speculative abstraction layers,
  configuration options or empty modules for imagined future needs, and no
  retries, compatibility layers or fallback branches for cases with no real
  trigger.
- Drive host applications by background script. Do not ask the user to click
  through a GUI to verify something a script can check. Stop only the host
  process your own run started, by its PID; the user may have their own
  instance open.
- Never write a machine-specific path into a tracked file, Issue or PR. Use
  placeholders such as `<scratch>`, `<samples>` or the host executable's name
  in angle brackets.
