## Default checks

Run from the repository root. Run the rows a change touches.

| Scope | Command |
| --- | --- |
| Documentation, rules and project files | `python tools/validate_project.py` |
| Project tools | `python -m unittest discover -s tests` |

<!-- 待定制：增补本项目的检查行，每行写范围和命令（例如 LoomlineRetopo：python -m ruff check .、python -m pytest tests、Blender 后台测试 <blender> -b --factory-startup --python ...；MtoULiveLink：mayapy 的 unittest、UnrealEditor-Cmd 的 Automation RunTests；StoUTextureSync：python -m compileall -q plugins）。宿主检查写明需要什么宿主，CI 实际覆盖哪些行 -->

- During iteration, rerun only the failing test or module. Run each affected
  suite once more when the change stabilizes.
- Automated tests use deterministic generated or hand-built fixtures. They
  never read `samples/` or use the network.
- Opt-in scripts (benchmarks, performance runs, host acceptance) run when a
  task needs visual acceptance, stage acceptance or a performance claim.
  Report their numbers with the machine and input size.
- A missing prerequisite (no host application, no samples) is reported as
  missing. Do not substitute a stand-in and claim the check passed.

## Data and privacy

- Production material lives only under the ignored `samples/` directory, one
  subdirectory per set with a neutral code name (`set-a`). Review packages for
  the user live only under the ignored `reviews/` directory. Never commit
  them, and never upload files or images derived from them to an Issue, a PR
  or any external service without the user's approval. Refer to sample sets by
  their neutral names.
- Never modify or overwrite a user input file. Commands write only to the
  output paths they are given.
- Keep scratch under the `.tmp/` directory beside the actual Git root
  (the root's parent directory plus `.tmp`); when that is not writable, use
  the ignored `.tmp/` inside this repository. Remove task-owned scratch when
  it is no longer needed.
- Never publish credentials, machine-specific paths or private asset contents
  in files, Issues or PRs.

## Session handoff

A session can end without warning: the user's usage allowance runs out, and
no session can see how much of it is left. Resuming or compacting a long
conversation afterwards is expensive. So the work is kept resumable at all
times by a fresh session that has only the repository and `HANDOFF.md`.

- Keep `HANDOFF.md` in the root of the working directory you are working in;
  each worktree has its own. Git ignores it: it is a note to the next
  session, not a project document. Knowledge that must last still goes to the
  Issue, docs/roadmap.md or an ADR.
- Update it at every checkpoint, without being asked and without waiting for
  the task to end: a step finished and verified, a decision made, an approach
  tried and ruled out, and before starting anything long (a full suite, a
  benchmark, a subagent, a review round). Do not try to guess when the
  allowance will run out; a note that is always current is the only
  protection.
- Save the work at the same checkpoints: commit verified work to the branch
  allowed by docs/workflow.md (the development branch for small rule or
  documentation edits, the task branch for PR work), so the note points at a
  commit. Anything left uncommitted is listed in the note with its state.
- Write it in Chinese, as the current state, rewritten in place and never
  appended to. It holds only what a fresh session cannot find in the
  repository, the Issues or the documents. Before writing a line, ask: would
  the next session go wrong without it, and can it not look this up? What can
  be looked up gets a pointer (commit, Issue, file), never a copy.
- Sections, each left out when it has nothing to say, except `进行中`:
  - `任务`: the goal, Issue number, branch, last commit SHA, update date.
  - `已完成`: progress on the task in flight that no commit message, Issue
    comment or PR yet records, and how it was verified. Remove an entry once
    it is merged or written to the Issue.
  - `进行中`: what is half-done, exactly where it stopped, uncommitted files;
    `无` when nothing is in flight.
  - `下一步`: ordered actions, concrete enough to start on directly.
  - `关键决定`: decisions taken and approaches ruled out on the task in
    flight, each with its reason, until they are recorded in the code, the
    Issue or an ADR.
  - `未验证`: checks the task in flight still needs before delivery, host
    acceptance included.
- State as fact only what this session observed or verified itself. A claim
  taken over from an earlier note, a subagent's report or a guess is either
  verified first or left out; do not pass it on, marked or unmarked.
- It does not hold: the history of merged work, descriptions of code or
  algorithms (those belong in the code and documents), lessons about how to
  work (project-wide ones go to this file or docs/workflow.md; a tool's own
  go to that tool's memory), or secrets and machine-specific paths.
- Keep it within about 40 lines while a task is in flight, and about 15 when
  none is: branch, last commit and `下一步`.
- At the start of a session, if `HANDOFF.md` exists, read it before anything
  else and compare it with `git status` and the recent log. Where they
  disagree, the repository is right: correct the note. Then continue from
  `下一步`; do not redo investigation the note already records.
- When the user asks to continue interrupted work, continue from
  `HANDOFF.md` in the current session; do not ask them to reopen the earlier
  conversation.
- When the task is delivered, cut the note down to branch, last commit and
  `下一步`. A stale note misleads the next session.

## Documentation and delivery

- Write documents, Issues and comments as the current state. When a decision
  changes, agree it with the user and rewrite it in place; leave out
  development diaries and change notes.
- README.md and README_CN.md are aligned current manuals for first-time users:
  what it is, compatibility, installation, workflows, troubleshooting and
  limits. Update the affected section in place; keep implementation, test
  counts and development narrative out.
- Each kind of content has one owner; other places link to it. Vocabulary in
  CONTEXT.md, requirements in docs/requirements.md, structure in
  docs/architecture.md, long-lived decisions in `docs/adr/`, plan and stage
  status in docs/roadmap.md, version changes in `CHANGELOG.md`. A change that
  makes a document's statement untrue rewrites that document in the same
  change.
- User-facing text (diagnostic messages, reports, UI labels, review notes) is
  Chinese. Code, identifiers, logs and commit messages are English.
- `main` holds only stable milestones and releases; task PRs never target it.
  Work lands on the development branch in progress (`dev/<topic>`): check which
  one it is before you start, and create one from `main` when none is. Task
  branches are `feature/<topic>` or `fix/<topic>`, cut from it with a PR back
  to it. Merging the development branch into `main` is a separate step the
  user agrees to. A PR branch holds only its PR's change; small documentation
  and rule edits go directly on the development branch as docs/workflow.md
  defines.
- Follow docs/workflow.md for Issue/PR work: `Refs #<issue>` (never closing
  keywords), full base and head SHAs, AC and `R-` finding IDs, a published
  review of the exact head SHA before every merge, `gh pr merge
  --match-head-commit` merges, and issue comments that each end with a
  `**当前状态**` footer. Comments stay short; one conclusion comment per review
  round, and a comment a later one supersedes is deleted. Status comments are
  generated and checked with `tools/issue_report.py`.
- Whoever wrote a change does not review it and does not re-verify its own fix:
  a PR is merged only after a review of its exact head commit by another
  session, another tool or the user. The implementer delivers the PR as
  **待审核** and stops there.
- A review finding needs evidence: an unmet completion criterion, a broken
  architecture rule or a reproducible defect. A preference for a different
  approach is not a finding. A recorded decision is reopened only with new
  evidence (docs/workflow.md section 6).
- Report the state reached precisely: implemented, reviewed, merged, visually
  accepted or released. Say which checks ran and which did not.

## Versions and changelog

Only the release change sets the product version; other PRs never change it.
The version starts at 0.1.0; the user decides when it becomes 1.0.0. Release
tags are `v<version>`; docs/workflow.md section 11 describes the release.

Write `CHANGELOG.md` for users upgrading. Every PR with a user-visible change
adds its entry under `## Unreleased`; the release change only renames that
heading to the version and date. Group entries under `### Upgrade notes`,
`### Added`, `### Changed`, `### Fixed` and `### Removed`, in that order,
omitting empty groups. Upgrade notes say what the user must do. Each entry is
one or two lines in the product's own terms and ends with its issue:

```markdown
- Long names no longer widen the panel; the full name is in the tooltip. (#71)
```

Leave out implementation, review finding IDs, tests and tooling; the issue and
PR hold them.
